      "side": 1,
      "message": "It\u0027s very interesting to me that marking the texture as dirty in beginCommandBuffer() when there is a color clear actually fixes this test in WebGL/WASM. I\u0027m wondering if it holds a clue to the Chrome issue. From my local testing on GL it seems like it shouldn\u0027t matter. The texture begins life \"dirty\" and calls to mark it dirty effectively are no-ops and then it gets \"resolved\" during export to GrBackendTexture. Moreover, the code that actually checks and does the resolve isn\u0027t even looking at the GrTexture state, just the proxy state. I\u0027m going to look into why this is different in WebGL.",
      "revId": "520550cac726905474afbd73e6c49294f26510c0",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "e3dfd2fc_0b5fdd68",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 4
